\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"width: 22.56%; font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Immune thrombocytopenia</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"width: 18.86%; font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">Normal</p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"width: 18.86%; font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">Normal</p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"width: 21.48%; font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">\u2193</p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"width: 18.26%; font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">\u2191</p></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" colspan=\"5\" style=\"width: 100%; font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">aPTT</strong> = <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">activated partial thromboplastin time; PT</strong> = prothrombin time.</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div></div></div></div><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">This patient with end-stage renal disease has symptoms of <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">uremia</strong> (eg, fatigue, nausea, pruritus) and excessive bleeding despite a normal platelet count.\u00a0 Bleeding is common in advanced <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">chronic kidney disease</strong> (CKD) due in part to the accumulation of uremic toxins, which <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">impair platelet adhesion and aggregation</strong>.\u00a0 Anemia, which is common in CKD due to decreased erythropoietin production, further impairs platelet function because red blood cells normally push platelets toward the vessel wall, where they adhere (margination).\u00a0 Typical laboratory findings in CKD include:</p><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Platelet count: usually normal or only mildly decreased.\u00a0 Platelet production is regulated mainly by thrombopoietin (produced primarily by the liver), so decreased erythropoietin production does not cause thrombocytopenia.</li></ul><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Bleeding time: a test of platelet function in which a standardized incision is made on the forearm, and the time to cessation of bleeding is measured.\u00a0 <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Prolonged bleeding time</strong> is characteristic of uremic platelet dysfunction.</li></ul><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Prothrombin time (PT): assesses effective levels of <a class=\"hint\" data-exhibit-id=\"exhibit-etkobdbkfaym4qj5eninhz6k\" href=\"#exhibit-exhibit-etkobdbkfaym4qj5eninhz6k\">clotting factors</a> in the extrinsic and common pathways (ie, factors VII, X, V, II, and fibrinogen).\u00a0 <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">PT is usually normal</strong> in CKD.</li></ul><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT): assesses effective levels of clotting factors in the intrinsic and common pathways (ie, factors XII, XI, IX, VIII, X, V, II, and fibrinogen).\u00a0 In patients with CKD, <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">aPTT is usually normal</strong>.</li></ul><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">Uremic bleeding can be <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">improved with dialysis</strong>, which removes the toxins and partially reverses the platelet inhibition; correcting anemia (eg, with erythropoiesis-stimulating agents) is also helpful.\u00a0 <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Desmopressin</strong>, which stimulates release of von Willebrand factor (vWF) from endothelial cells, can be used to treat acute uremic bleeding.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice B)</strong>\u00a0 vWF mediates <a class=\"hint\" data-exhibit-id=\"exhibit-mwvwzm7du61tnsx396a335tp\" href=\"#exhibit-exhibit-mwvwzm7du61tnsx396a335tp\">platelet adhesion</a> to the vessel wall; deficiency results in prolonged bleeding time with a normal platelet count.\u00a0 Because vWF also protects factor VIII from degradation, vWF deficiency can result in decreased factor VIII and prolonged aPTT.\u00a0 In contrast, uremic platelet dysfunction does not affect factor VIII levels, so aPTT is normal.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice C)</strong>\u00a0 Isolated aPTT prolongation is seen with hemophilia A, an X-linked disorder caused by factor VIII deficiency.\u00a0 Unfractionated heparin prolongs aPTT via antithrombin-associated inhibition of factors IIa (thrombin) and Xa (PT is usually normal due to neutralizers in the PT reagent).</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice D)</strong>\u00a0 Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) is a consumptive coagulopathy associated with decreased platelet count and increased bleeding time, aPTT, and PT.\u00a0 Oozing from vascular access sites is classic for DIC but can occur with any significant hemostatic defect.\u00a0 DIC is unlikely in this patient given his normal platelet count and the absence of a triggering condition (eg, sepsis, malignancy, trauma).</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice E)</strong>\u00a0 Warfarin inhibits vitamin K epoxide reductase, decreasing synthesis of functional vitamin K-dependent clotting factors (II, VII, IX, and X).\u00a0 Warfarin prolongs PT and can also prolong aPTT when vitamin K-dependent clotting factor levels are sufficiently reduced.\u00a0 Platelet count and bleeding time are normal.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Educational objective:</strong>Uremic toxins in patients with advanced chronic kidney disease can cause impaired platelet function with abnormal bleeding.\u00a0 Bleeding time is prolonged, but platelet count, prothrombin time, and activated partial thromboplastin time are typically normal.\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c</p></span></div>", "educationalObjective": "Uremic toxins in patients with advanced chronic kidney disease can cause impaired platelet function with abnormal bleeding.  Bleeding time is prolonged, but platelet count, prothrombin time, and activated partial thromboplastin time are typically normal.\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c", "subject": "Pathology", "system": "Hematology & Oncology", "topic": "Coagulopathy", "category": "Hemostasis and t